#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Publish the branch a QA minion accepted: push it, and open the merge request.

Opening a merge request is exact. The branch is named on the QA task, the target is
named on the project, and the forge is whatever `origin` points at. No step of it
needs understanding, so it lives in a script rather than in instructions an agent
has to be trusted to follow.

What a human reads on it is not exact, and is not made here. The ship minion writes
it at `$SIANA_HOME/handoffs/<ship-id>.md` once its work is finished, and
`siana-handoff` validates that document and assembles the title and body out of it.
This passes the head QA accepted, so a copy describing an earlier commit refuses the
publish rather than travelling with work it does not describe. Nothing here asks a
model: publishing stays a deterministic assembly of prose an agent already wrote and
a second minion already accepted.

Merging is the captain's, unless their registry record for the project says
otherwise. `automerge` is that record: absent, this reaches the push target and stops,
which is what every project did before the field existed. Set to `merge`, `squash` or
`rebase`, it is standing permission to *arrange* the merge of exactly the head an
independent minion accepted, by exactly that method - and arranging is all it is,
because what decides whether the merge happens is still the forge's own required
checks and required reviews.

The grant is a fourth authority and replaces none of the other three. It composes:
`pipeline` says a run validated a commit, `qa` says a second minion accepted that same
commit, `target` says where it lands, and only then does this ask the forge whether
its own gate is green on that same commit. A project carrying the grant without those
three refuses to publish at all, because a grant honoured on fewer conditions than the
captain set is not the grant they gave.

Nothing is inferred into it. Not from a previous merge, not from the repository's
settings, not from the shape of a branch, and not from anything an agent said. The
field is the only place it is written, and the captain owns that file.

What is armed outlives this process and the registry both. Removing the field stops
the next publish from arming anything and cannot retract what a forge already holds,
so `--cancel-automerge` exists and is exact: it disarms the open request and then asks
again to prove it is disarmed. Revoking the grant safely is cancel first, check, then
remove the field, in that order.

It takes the QA task and never the ship task. A rejected ship task is repaired on a
new branch cut from the old one, so after one rejection `siana/<ship-id>` is no
longer the branch worth publishing and a script starting there would have a chain to
walk. A QA task's `base` is the branch that QA actually read, so reading it publishes
exactly the head a second minion accepted, and makes publishing something QA never
saw unrepresentable rather than merely discouraged.

Re-running is safe and is the intended recovery. Publishing happens outside the
store, so a SIANA that restarts between a QA verdict and this call cannot know
whether the merge request exists; asking the forge is cheaper than inventing
somewhere to record it, so an existing merge request is reported and exits zero.

A repair of already published work publishes the other way. Its brief records the
branch that work was published from, so instead of opening a second request holding
the same commits, this fast-forwards that branch to the head QA accepted and the
repair arrives on the thread the captain is already reading. The record is stated by
SIANA at briefing time and never inferred here: every fix is cut from the branch it
repairs, so ancestry, a title or a request that happens to be open over there would
make ordinary work land on somebody else's review.

The copy on that request is rewritten from the repair's own handoff, for the reason
the same-branch path rewrites one: what a reviewer is reading has to describe the
commits under it, and after a repair those are the repair's. So a repair minion writes
a standalone handoff - what the work was for, and what the repaired solution now does -
and it is validated against the head QA accepted before anything is pushed.

**While an advisory session is in force, this asks `siana-gate` before it pushes.**
An advisory session permits nothing, so what actually happens is that the proposal is
written into the captain's decision ledger - the exact command, the evidence, the
principles SIANA cited, what it rejected - and then this refuses, before any network
write. `--record` is where SIANA writes that proposal, and under a session it is
required: a publish nobody can say the reasoning for is not one the captain can judge
in the morning. The gate's own exit code is passed through, so a caller can still tell
a refusal from a fault.

**Passing `--record` is itself a request to be gated**, whether or not a session turns
out to be running. A caller only writes a decision record because it believes it is
under one, and the moment that belief can be wrong is the moment it matters: a session
whose deadline passes at 06:00 releases its record, and a SIANA woken at 06:01 that
had already written its proposal would otherwise push the branch and open the merge
request with nothing recorded anywhere. So a record is always carried to the gate, and
the gate answers a missing session by writing the proposal and refusing.

**With no advisory session, nothing here changes.** The captain typed this command, or
told SIANA to, and that is the authority it has always run on. A safety feature that
made a direct instruction impossible would be a worse fleet, not a safer one.
"""

import argparse
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys


def die(message: str, *hints: str):
    print(f"error: {message}", file=sys.stderr)
    for hint in hints:
        print(f"  {hint}", file=sys.stderr)
    raise SystemExit(1)


def fold(store: str, key: str):
    """The last record for each key in an append-only store.

    Reads only. Every write still goes through `tasks` or `datafile`, so what is on
    disk has already passed its contract. A tombstone is a last line like any other:
    a dropped record must read as absent, not as a record with every field missing.

    A store with no writes yet has no file at all, which is empty rather than a
    fault."""
    out = {}
    if not os.path.isfile(store):
        return out
    with open(store) as fh:
        for line in fh:
            line = line.strip()
            if not line:
                continue
            rec = json.loads(line)
            rid = rec.get(key)
            if rid is None:
                continue
            if rec.get("_deleted"):
                out.pop(rid, None)
            else:
                out[rid] = rec
    return out


# The one line in a ship brief that a script reads. Indented and a single token, so
# prose about the branch cannot be mistaken for the record of it.
BRANCH_LINE = re.compile(r"^[ \t]+branch[ \t]+(\S+)[ \t]*$", re.M)

# Only a ship brief names a ship branch. A QA brief carries the branch it judges in
# the same shape, and reading that as the QA task's own would put its minion on the
# branch it was meant to be cut from rather than beside it.
SHIP_SECTION = re.compile(r"^## Delivery: ship[ \t]*$(.*?)(?=^## |\Z)", re.M | re.S)

# `siana/<task-id>`, or `siana/<type>/<task-id>`. Shape only: which types are allowed
# is `siana-brief`'s to say, because that is the one place a type is ever stated, and
# a second opinion here would be a second place to change when that list moves.
BRANCH_NAME = re.compile(r"siana/[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]*(?:/[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]*)?")


def ship_branch(home: str, task_id: str) -> str:
    """The branch this task's work lives on.

    Ship work is briefed with an explicit Conventional Commit type, and `siana-brief`
    writes the `siana/<type>/<task-id>` that follows from it into the brief's own
    delivery section. That line is the fleet's only record of the type, because the
    type is stated and never inferred: rebuilding a branch name here out of a title,
    a diff or a commit is the guess the whole convention exists to prevent.

    A brief that names no branch answers `siana/<task-id>`. That covers scout and QA
    work, whose branch names are fleet roles rather than commit types, and every task
    briefed before this existed, so nothing already in flight has to be migrated to
    stay dispatchable.

    A line this cannot read refuses instead of falling back. Every caller is asking
    which branch holds the work, and answering `siana/<task-id>` when the brief says
    otherwise would put a worktree, a review or a publish on a branch nobody named.
    """
    path = os.path.join(home, "briefs", f"{task_id}.md")
    try:
        with open(path) as fh:
            section = SHIP_SECTION.search(fh.read())
    except OSError:
        return f"siana/{task_id}"
    named = sorted(set(BRANCH_LINE.findall(section.group(1)))) if section else []
    if not named:
        return f"siana/{task_id}"
    if len(named) > 1:
        die(f"{path} names more than one branch: {', '.join(named)}",
            "a brief records exactly one, and which of them holds the work is not",
            "something a script may choose between; fix the brief")
    if not BRANCH_NAME.fullmatch(named[0]):
        die(f"{path} records the branch as {named[0]!r}",
            "a fleet branch is `siana/<type>/<task-id>` for ship work and",
            "`siana/<task-id>` otherwise; nothing here may guess at what was meant")
    return named[0]


# The second line a repair's ship brief records, beside the branch. It names the ship
# task whose published work this repairs, and the branch that work was published
# from, in that order and on one line, so an accepted repair lands on the request
# that is already open instead of opening a second one for the same work.
REPAIR_LINE = re.compile(r"^[ \t]+repairs[ \t]+(\S+)[ \t]+(\S+)[ \t]*$", re.M)

# Anything indented that even claims to be that record. What it catches is a record
# the strict line cannot read: half of it, three of it, a name wearing a space. Read
# as no record at all, such a brief publishes a second request for work that already
# has one, which is the exact failure the record exists to prevent, so it stops here
# instead.
REPAIR_CLAIM = re.compile(r"^[ \t]+repairs\b.*$", re.M)

# A task id as the store's own contract writes one. Compared rather than trusted: a
# record naming something else is a record nothing can look up.
TASK_ID = re.compile(r"[a-z][a-z0-9-]*")


def repair_record(home: str, task_id: str):
    """The ship task this task repairs and the branch that work was published from,
    or None when this is ordinary ship work.

    Stated by SIANA at briefing time and never inferred here. A repair is not
    readable off prose, a title, a commit message, ancestry, or the forge: a branch
    cut from another one is the ordinary shape of every fix, and a request that
    happens to be open over there is not evidence that this work belongs on it.

    Every unreadable shape refuses rather than answering None. None means ordinary
    ship work, which opens a request of its own, so answering it about a brief that
    says `repairs` is how the duplicate this record exists to prevent gets opened."""
    path = os.path.join(home, "briefs", f"{task_id}.md")
    try:
        with open(path) as fh:
            section = SHIP_SECTION.search(fh.read())
    except OSError:
        return None
    if section is None:
        return None
    claimed = REPAIR_CLAIM.findall(section.group(1))
    if not claimed:
        return None
    named = REPAIR_LINE.findall(section.group(1))
    if len(claimed) > 1 or len(named) != 1:
        die(f"{path} does not record exactly one repair",
            *[f"  {line.strip()}" for line in claimed],
            "a repair record is one line of `repairs <ship-task> <branch>`, and",
            "which request an accepted repair lands on is not something a script",
            "may choose between; fix the brief")
    target, branch = named[0]
    if not TASK_ID.fullmatch(target) or not BRANCH_NAME.fullmatch(branch):
        die(f"{path} records the repair as {target!r} {branch!r}",
            "it names the ship task whose work this repairs and the branch that",
            "work was published from; nothing here may guess at what was meant")
    return target, branch


def publication_branch(home: str, task_id: str) -> str:
    """The branch whose merge request this task's work is published on.

    Its own branch, unless it is itself a repair: a repair of a repair belongs on the
    same request as the work it descends from, and the record carries that branch
    already, so the chain is one hop long however many repairs deep the work is."""
    record = repair_record(home, task_id)
    return record[1] if record else ship_branch(home, task_id)


def git(path: str, *args, check=True):
    out = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", path, *args], capture_output=True, text=True)
    if check and out.returncode != 0:
        die(f"git {' '.join(args)} failed in {path}", (out.stderr or out.stdout).strip())
    return out


def forge_of(remote_url: str):
    """Which forge `origin` lives on.

    Derived rather than configured: a field would be one more thing to set and one
    more thing to go stale against a remote that moved. An unrecognised host stops
    here instead of guessing a CLI, because guessing wrong means either a command
    that does not exist or one that publishes somewhere nobody asked for."""
    if "gitlab" in remote_url:
        return "gitlab"
    if "github" in remote_url:
        return "github"
    return None


def existing(forge: str, path: str, branch: str):
    """The open merge request for this branch, or None.

    Asked before every create, so re-running after an interrupted publish reports
    what is already there rather than opening a second one."""
    if forge == "gitlab":
        out = subprocess.run(
            ["glab", "mr", "list", "--source-branch", branch, "--output", "json"],
            cwd=path, capture_output=True, text=True)
        if out.returncode != 0:
            return None
        try:
            found = json.loads(out.stdout or "[]")
        except json.JSONDecodeError:
            return None
        return found[0].get("web_url") if found else None
    out = subprocess.run(
        ["gh", "pr", "list", "--head", branch, "--state", "open", "--json", "url"],
        cwd=path, capture_output=True, text=True)
    if out.returncode != 0:
        return None
    try:
        found = json.loads(out.stdout or "[]")
    except json.JSONDecodeError:
        return None
    return found[0].get("url") if found else None


def create(forge: str, path: str, branch: str, target: str, title: str, body: str):
    if forge == "gitlab":
        argv = ["glab", "mr", "create", "--source-branch", branch,
                "--target-branch", target, "--title", title,
                "--description", body, "--yes", "--no-editor"]
    else:
        argv = ["gh", "pr", "create", "--head", branch, "--base", target,
                "--title", title, "--body", body]
    out = subprocess.run(argv, cwd=path, capture_output=True, text=True)
    if out.returncode != 0:
        die(f"{forge} refused to open the merge request for {branch}",
            (out.stderr or out.stdout).strip(),
            "the branch is pushed either way, so nothing is lost by fixing this",
            "and running this command again")
    for word in (out.stdout + " " + out.stderr).split():
        if word.startswith("http"):
            return word.strip()
    return out.stdout.strip()


def copy(ship_id: str, head: str, qa_id: str):
    """The title and the body a human reads, from the handoff the ship minion wrote.

    Resolved beside this script for the reason `siana-gate` is: a `siana-handoff`
    that is not there has to be a refusal, never a merge request opened with
    whatever description a lookup that came back empty left behind.

    Its stderr is not captured, so its refusal reaches whoever is reading in its own
    words: it is the one place that knows which rule the document broke, and a
    caller paraphrasing it would decide half of it on the way past. Its exit code is
    passed through, so a malformed handoff and a fault stay distinguishable.

    The head is passed rather than looked up here, so a copy written against an
    earlier commit stops the publish. `--accepted-by` is the one thing a script adds
    to a body: that a second agent exercised this and accepted it is a fact about
    the queue, and the minion that wrote the copy finished before any of it
    happened."""
    handoff = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "siana-handoff")
    if not os.path.isfile(handoff):
        die(f"there is no {handoff}",
            "it is what turns the ship minion's handoff into a title and a body, so",
            "without it there is nothing to describe this work with",
            "run `just init` in the distro")
    out = subprocess.run([handoff, ship_id, "--head", head, "--accepted-by", qa_id,
                          "--json"], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, text=True)
    if out.returncode != 0:
        print("nothing was pushed and no merge request was opened", file=sys.stderr)
        raise SystemExit(out.returncode)
    made = json.loads(out.stdout)
    return made["title"], made["body"]


def update(forge: str, path: str, branch: str, title: str, body: str):
    """Put the accepted copy onto a merge request that is already open.

    Re-running is the intended recovery from an interrupted publish, and the copy
    can have moved between the two runs: a rejected round is repaired, the handoff
    is rewritten against the new head, and the second run is the one carrying what
    QA actually accepted. Reporting the open merge request and leaving it as it was
    would make that the one run whose copy never arrived.

    It overwrites whatever description is there, including one a human typed. That
    is the tradeoff taken deliberately: the description of a merge request the fleet
    opened is generated from the handoff every run, so an edit that should survive
    belongs in the handoff, and copy that silently disagreed with what QA accepted
    would be the worse of the two failures."""
    if forge == "gitlab":
        argv = ["glab", "mr", "update", branch, "--title", title,
                "--description", body]
    else:
        argv = ["gh", "pr", "edit", branch, "--title", title, "--body", body]
    out = subprocess.run(argv, cwd=path, capture_output=True, text=True)
    if out.returncode != 0:
        die(f"{forge} refused to update the merge request for {branch}",
            (out.stderr or out.stdout).strip(),
            "the branch is pushed and the merge request is open, so it is still",
            "there to read; it is carrying copy from an earlier run")


# What each forge is asked for the requests on one branch, and what it calls the
# three fields this needs. Every state is asked for rather than the open ones only:
# a request that was closed or merged is a different answer from no request at all,
# and advancing a branch nobody is reading is not something to do quietly.
REQUESTS = {
    "github": (["gh", "pr", "list", "--head", "{branch}", "--state", "all",
                "--json", "url,state,headRefName,headRefOid,baseRefName,isDraft,"
                          "mergeable,autoMergeRequest"],
               {"url": "url", "state": "state",
                "head": "headRefOid", "source": "headRefName",
                "target": "baseRefName", "draft": "isDraft",
                "mergeable": "mergeable", "armed": "autoMergeRequest"}),
    "gitlab": (["glab", "mr", "list", "--source-branch", "{branch}", "--all",
                "--output", "json"],
               {"url": "web_url", "state": "state",
                "head": "sha", "source": "source_branch",
                "target": "target_branch", "draft": "draft",
                "mergeable": "detailed_merge_status",
                "armed": "auto_merge_enabled"}),
}

def request_field(name, value):
    """One field of a request as this reads it.

    Three of them are not strings. `draft` is a bool on both clients, `armed` is an
    object on one and a bool on the other, and `mergeable` is the one whose two
    spellings mean genuinely different things - github answers MERGEABLE or
    CONFLICTING, gitlab answers a much longer status with its own vocabulary. Nothing
    compares `mergeable` across the two, because only github is ever asked to arrange
    a merge; on gitlab it is carried for the same reason `armed` is, so that a report
    can say what the forge holds without a second call."""
    if name == "draft":
        return bool(value)
    if name == "armed":
        return armed_with(value)
    return str(value or "")


def armed_with(value):
    """The method an open request is already armed to merge with, or "" when it is
    not armed at all.

    github answers with the whole auto-merge record or with null, gitlab with a bool.
    Reduced to one string here so nothing downstream has to know which, and so a
    request armed with a method other than the configured one reads as different
    rather than as done."""
    if isinstance(value, dict):
        return str(value.get("mergeMethod") or "armed").lower()
    if value is True:
        return "armed"
    return ""

# github says OPEN, gitlab says opened. Both also have states this must not treat as
# one: a merged request whose branch is advanced afterwards is work pushed at
# something nobody will read again.
OPEN = {"open", "opened"}


def requests_for(forge: str, path: str, branch: str):
    """Every merge request whose source is this branch, or why none could be asked.

    The reason travels back instead of an empty list. A forge that could not answer
    and a branch that has no request are the same shape and opposite facts: one is
    work waiting for its first request, the other is a request this is about to push
    into, and `existing` above conflates them on purpose because opening a second
    request is recoverable. Advancing somebody's branch is not."""
    argv, fields = REQUESTS[forge]
    argv = [part.replace("{branch}", branch) for part in argv]
    out = subprocess.run(argv, cwd=path, capture_output=True, text=True)
    if out.returncode != 0:
        return None, ((out.stderr or out.stdout).strip()
                      or f"{argv[0]} exited {out.returncode} and said nothing")
    try:
        found = json.loads(out.stdout or "[]")
    except json.JSONDecodeError:
        return None, f"{argv[0]} answered something that is not JSON"
    if not isinstance(found, list) or any(not isinstance(r, dict) for r in found):
        return None, f"{argv[0]} answered something that is not a list of requests"
    listed = [{name: request_field(name, r.get(key))
               for name, key in fields.items()}
              for r in found]
    # Filtered here as well as asked for. The source branch is the whole of what ties
    # a request to this work, and a client that widened its own matching would hand
    # back a request about somebody else's branch with nothing here to notice.
    return [r for r in listed if r["source"] == branch], None


def held(tasks_file: str, handle: str, branch: str):
    """The live task whose minion is sitting on this branch, or None.

    Asked of the queue and the worktree together, because neither answers alone: a
    `doing` task says a minion is working, and the branch its worktree is on says
    what it is working on. Scoped to this project, so a branch of the same name in
    another repository is somebody else's.

    Pushing at a branch a minion is committing to leaves that minion diverged from
    the request it is meant to be repairing, and it finds out at its own publish."""
    for task_id, task in sorted(fold(tasks_file, "id").items()):
        if task.get("status") != "doing" or task.get("project") != handle:
            continue
        cwd = task.get("cwd")
        if not cwd or not os.path.isdir(cwd):
            continue
        on = git(cwd, "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD", check=False)
        if on.returncode == 0 and on.stdout.strip() == branch:
            return task_id, task
    return None


# The forges a standing `automerge` grant is honoured on, and the three calls each
# one needs spelled as that forge's client spells them.
#
# Only github is here, and the absence of gitlab is the boundary rather than a gap
# waiting to be filled. Two of the three have no `glab` spelling at all. There is no
# subcommand that cancels an armed merge, so arming one would create external state
# this fleet has no exact way to retract. And there is no list of the checks a gitlab
# project requires: a merge request there has one pipeline, and whether it has to be
# green is a project setting the client does not report, so "this branch has no
# required check" and "this project requires nothing" arrive as the same empty
# answer. Reading that as a green is the exact failure the grant is written to make
# impossible, so gitlab is refused under it and publishes as it always did.
AUTOMERGE = {
    "github": {
        "checks": ["gh", "pr", "checks", "{branch}", "--required",
                   "--json", "name,state,bucket"],
        "arm": ["gh", "pr", "merge", "{branch}", "--auto", "{method}",
                "--match-head-commit", "{head}"],
        "cancel": ["gh", "pr", "merge", "{branch}", "--disable-auto"],
        # The client's word for each method the contract allows. Passed through and
        # never defaulted: a script choosing between these would be choosing how the
        # captain's history reads.
        "methods": {"merge": "--merge", "squash": "--squash", "rebase": "--rebase"},
    },
}

# What `gh pr checks` calls a check that has already decided against the work. A
# pending one may still arm - waiting for CI is the whole reason to arm at all - and
# a skipped one is a check the forge itself is not holding the merge for.
CHECK_FAILED = {"fail", "cancel"}


def pipeline_run(home: str, task_id: str):
    """The passing `siana-pipeline` run recorded for this task, or why there is none.

    Read exactly as `siana-pipeline check` reads it and never re-run: a run validates
    one commit and writes down which, and the whole use of it here is comparing that
    commit against the head an independent minion accepted."""
    path = os.path.join(home, "pipeline", f"{task_id}.json")
    if not os.path.isfile(path):
        return None, f"there is no run recorded at {path}"
    try:
        with open(path) as fh:
            record = json.load(fh)
    except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError) as e:
        return None, f"{path} cannot be read: {e}"
    if record.get("verdict") != "passed":
        return None, f"the run at {path} did not pass ({record.get('verdict')})"
    return record, None


def automerge_grant(home: str, registry: str, handle: str, project, forge: str,
                    ship_id: str, branch: str, accepted: str) -> None:
    """Refuse unless every standing condition for a delegated merge holds here.

    All of it is about this registry and this machine, so all of it is known before
    anything is pushed and all of it refuses there. A project carrying the grant with
    a prerequisite missing refuses the publish outright rather than publishing and
    quietly arming nothing: the captain set the field to say work here merges once
    it is accepted, and a run that pushed the branch and left the merge unarranged
    would read afterwards as the grant having been honoured.

    The grant is a fourth authority and stands in for none of the other three. `qa`
    is what makes a second minion's acceptance a condition rather than a habit;
    `pipeline` is what makes a run's verdict bound to a commit rather than to a
    branch; `target` is what makes publishing happen at all. Cumulative and never
    substitutable: a local green says nothing about what QA will find, and a QA green
    says nothing about what the forge's own checks will find on the published head.
    """
    if forge not in AUTOMERGE:
        die(f"{handle} grants automerge, and this fleet does not arrange merges "
            f"on {forge}",
            "there is no client call there that cancels an armed merge, and none",
            "that says which checks the project requires, so an armed merge could",
            "not be retracted and an empty answer could not be told from a green",
            f"  datafile -f {registry} get {handle}   # then put it back",
            "  ... without --set automerge=...",
            "then publish again and merge it in person")

    if not project.get("pipeline"):
        die(f"{handle} grants automerge and is not validated by a driven pipeline",
            "the grant merges on the strength of a run bound to one commit, and a",
            "verify that runs once at `done` records no commit to bind to",
            f"set `pipeline` on {handle}, or drop `automerge` and merge in person")

    if not project.get("qa"):
        die(f"{handle} grants automerge and has no `qa` command",
            "the grant delegates arranging a merge, not deciding one; what decides",
            "is a second minion, and without `qa` no ship task here gets one",
            f"set `qa` on {handle}, or drop `automerge` and merge in person")

    record, why = pipeline_run(home, ship_id)
    if record is None:
        die(f"{ship_id} has no passing pipeline run to merge on", why,
            "the grant is standing permission to merge work this project's own",
            "rigor accepted, and nothing here says it did")
    if record.get("branch") != branch:
        die(f"the run recorded for {ship_id} validated "
            f"{record.get('branch')}, not {branch}",
            "it is a verdict about some other branch, so it says nothing about the",
            "work an independent minion accepted here")
    if record.get("head") != accepted:
        die(f"the run recorded for {ship_id} validated "
            f"{str(record.get('head'))[:12]}, and {branch} is at {accepted[:12]}",
            "the branch moved after the run, so the head QA accepted is one this",
            "project's own rigor never saw; run the pipeline again over what is",
            "there, have it accepted again, then publish")


def required_checks(forge: str, path: str, branch: str):
    """The checks this branch's request has to pass before the forge will merge it,
    or why nothing could be asked.

    The exit code is deliberately not read. The client exits nonzero when a required
    check has failed, when the branch has no check at all, and when it could not ask
    at all, and it exits 8 while one is still running - so no exit code here is a
    verdict about anything. What it printed is: a list of checks is an answer, and
    everything else - a login page, an error object, the client's own "no checks
    reported", silence - is not one.

    Both of those refuse upstream, which is why this does not try to tell an empty
    answer from an unreachable forge by reading the client's English. What it does
    keep apart is the two things a list can say: an empty list is a request nothing
    is required of, and that is a fact about a protection rule rather than about a
    client. Neither is ever a green."""
    argv = [part.replace("{branch}", branch) for part in AUTOMERGE[forge]["checks"]]
    out = subprocess.run(argv, cwd=path, capture_output=True, text=True)
    try:
        found = json.loads(out.stdout or "null")
    except json.JSONDecodeError:
        found = None
    if not isinstance(found, list) or any(not isinstance(c, dict) for c in found):
        return None, ((out.stderr or out.stdout).strip()
                      or f"{argv[0]} exited {out.returncode} and said nothing")
    return [{"name": str(c.get("name") or "?"),
             "state": str(c.get("state") or ""),
             "bucket": str(c.get("bucket") or "").lower()} for c in found], None


def one_request(forge: str, path: str, branch: str, accepted: str):
    """The open request from this branch, the merged one holding the accepted head,
    or why there is neither.

    Answers `(kind, request, refusal)`. A merged request at the accepted head is an
    end state rather than a problem: the grant did what it was set to do, and a
    re-run after the forge merged has to report that instead of hunting for
    something to arm."""
    found, problem = requests_for(forge, path, branch)
    if problem is not None:
        return None, None, (
            f"{forge} could not say what is open from {branch}", problem,
            "a merge is arranged against a request that exists, so a forge that",
            "cannot be asked is a refusal and never an assumption about one")
    merged = [r for r in found
              if r["state"].lower() == "merged" and r["head"] == accepted]
    if merged:
        return "merged", merged[0], None
    open_now = [r for r in found if r["state"].lower() in OPEN]
    if not open_now:
        return None, None, (
            f"no request is open from {branch}",
            *[f"  {r['state']}: {r['url']}" for r in found],
            "there is nothing to arrange the merge of, and a closed or merged",
            "request holding some other head is not this work")
    if len(open_now) > 1:
        return None, None, (
            f"{branch} is the source of more than one open request",
            *[f"  {r['url']}" for r in open_now],
            "which one an accepted head merges on is the captain's to say, and not",
            "something a script may choose between")
    return "open", open_now[0], None


def automerge_state(forge: str, path: str, branch: str, target: str, method: str,
                    accepted: str, pushed: bool = True):
    """Whether this request may be armed, and what it is carrying.

    Answers `(verdict, request, checks, refusal)`, acts on nothing, and is the whole
    of the decision - so the dry run explains what a real run would do by asking the
    same question. `merged` and `armed` are end states, which is what makes a re-run
    a report rather than a repetition.

    The accepted head is asked for three separate times: the request's, the remote
    branch's, and the request's again once the checks have been read. A branch that
    moves anywhere in that window is work no independent minion accepted, and the
    checks just read describe a commit that is no longer the one being merged. The
    arming call carries the head a fourth time, so the forge refuses it too.

    `pushed` is false for a dry run, which happens before the push and therefore
    before any of that is true yet: the request is at whatever the last publish left
    it at, and the remote may not have the branch at all. Comparing then would refuse
    every dry run of work that has not been published, which is exactly the run a
    captain does before a project's first publish. The three head comparisons are the
    only thing it turns off, and what still holds without them is that a real run
    makes all three and the forge makes a fourth."""
    kind, request, refusal = one_request(forge, path, branch, accepted)
    if refusal is not None:
        return "refuse", None, None, refusal
    if kind == "merged":
        return "merged", request, None, None

    if request["target"] and request["target"] != target:
        return "refuse", request, None, (
            f"{request['url']} targets {request['target']}, and {branch} publishes "
            f"to {target}",
            "the grant is permission to merge into the branch the registry names,",
            "and this would merge somewhere else")
    if request["draft"]:
        return "refuse", request, None, (
            f"{request['url']} is a draft",
            "a draft is a request its author has said is not ready, and the grant",
            "does not overrule that")
    # github's word, and the only one compared here: gitlab is refused above, so
    # its own longer status is never read as a verdict.
    if request["mergeable"].lower() == "conflicting":
        return "refuse", request, None, (
            f"{request['url']} conflicts with {target}",
            "resolving it changes the commits, so what merged would not be what an",
            "independent minion accepted")
    if pushed and request["head"] != accepted:
        at = request["head"][:12] or "a head it would not name"
        return "refuse", request, None, (
            f"{request['url']} is at {at}, and the accepted head is "
            f"{accepted[:12]}",
            "a request whose head cannot be tied to the accepted commit is one",
            "this cannot say it would be merging the work that was accepted")

    if pushed:
        ls = git(path, "ls-remote", "origin", f"refs/heads/{branch}", check=False)
        if ls.returncode != 0 or not ls.stdout.strip():
            return "refuse", request, None, (
                f"origin could not be read for {branch}",
                (ls.stderr or ls.stdout).strip() or f"origin has no {branch}",
                "the remote head is the branch the forge would merge, so nothing is",
                "armed until it can be read")
        if ls.stdout.split()[0] != accepted:
            return "refuse", request, None, (
                f"origin has {branch} at {ls.stdout.split()[0][:12]}, and the "
                f"accepted head is {accepted[:12]}",
                "somebody pushed after the verdict, so arming this would merge work",
                "no independent minion has read")

    checks, problem = required_checks(forge, path, branch)
    if problem is not None:
        return "refuse", request, None, (
            f"nothing said which checks {branch} requires on {accepted[:12]}",
            f"{forge} said: {problem}",
            "either this branch has no check at all, or the client could not be",
            "asked; both refuse here, because an answer that is not a list of",
            "checks is not an empty list of them and neither is ever a green",
            "the request is open and holds the accepted head, so if it was opened",
            "a moment ago its checks may not have started yet: run this again once",
            "they have")
    if not checks:
        return "refuse", request, [], (
            f"{branch} has no required check on {accepted[:12]}",
            "the grant merges when the forge's own gate passes, and a branch with",
            "no required check has no gate to wait for; an empty list is never a",
            "green, it is a protection rule that requires nothing",
            f"add a required check to the rule on {target}, or drop the grant and",
            "merge this one in person")

    # Asked again, after the checks and before anything is armed. A branch that moved
    # while the checks were being read leaves an answer about a commit that is no
    # longer the one this would merge, and that window is the only one the two reads
    # above cannot cover.
    kind, again, refusal = one_request(forge, path, branch, accepted)
    if refusal is not None:
        return "refuse", request, checks, refusal
    if kind == "merged":
        return "merged", again, checks, None
    if pushed and again["head"] != accepted:
        return "refuse", again, checks, (
            f"{branch} moved to {again['head'][:12]} while its checks were being "
            f"read",
            f"the accepted head is {accepted[:12]}, so those checks describe a",
            "commit that is not the one this would merge")

    if again["armed"] == method:
        return "armed", again, checks, None

    failed = [c for c in checks if c["bucket"] in CHECK_FAILED]
    if failed:
        return "refuse", again, checks, (
            f"{branch} has a required check that did not pass on {accepted[:12]}",
            *[f"  {c['bucket']:8} {c['name']}" for c in failed],
            "the forge would not merge it, and arming a request that cannot merge",
            "records an intention nobody will see fail")
    return "arm", again, checks, None


def show_checks(checks) -> str:
    """The required checks on one line, each with what it is doing.

    Named rather than counted: "3 required checks" reads the same whether they are
    green or three minutes from failing, and the whole reason a captain reads this
    is to know which."""
    if checks is None:
        # Either the run stopped before asking, or the answer was not a list of
        # checks. Both are said in the refusal printed under this line, so this says
        # only that the line above it is not a green.
        return "unreadable here (see below)"
    if not checks:
        return "none"
    return ", ".join(f"{c['name']} ({c['bucket'] or c['state'] or '?'})"
                     for c in checks)


def arm(forge: str, path: str, branch: str, method: str, accepted: str) -> None:
    """Arrange the merge, pinned to the accepted head and the configured method.

    The forge holds it until every required check and every required review passes,
    which is the reason this is a standing arrangement rather than something SIANA
    waits for: a session that polled CI would have to stay alive across it, and the
    thing that must not be lost when it does not is the binding to this commit."""
    spec = AUTOMERGE[forge]
    argv = [part.replace("{branch}", branch)
                .replace("{method}", spec["methods"][method])
                .replace("{head}", accepted)
            for part in spec["arm"]]
    out = subprocess.run(argv, cwd=path, capture_output=True, text=True)
    if out.returncode != 0:
        die(f"{forge} refused to arrange the merge of {branch}",
            (out.stderr or out.stdout).strip(),
            "the request is open and holds the accepted head, so nothing is lost:",
            "it is simply not armed, and merging it stays the captain's")


def disarm(forge: str, path: str, branch: str) -> None:
    spec = AUTOMERGE[forge]
    argv = [part.replace("{branch}", branch) for part in spec["cancel"]]
    out = subprocess.run(argv, cwd=path, capture_output=True, text=True)
    if out.returncode != 0:
        die(f"{forge} refused to cancel the armed merge of {branch}",
            (out.stderr or out.stdout).strip(),
            "the request is still armed, so it may still merge; cancel it in the",
            "forge's own interface before removing the grant")


def automerge_step(args, path: str, forge: str, branch: str, target: str,
                   method: str, accepted: str, qa_id: str, missing: bool) -> None:
    """Arrange the merge of the request this publish just put the accepted head on.

    Called after the push on both publication paths and never before one: what is
    armed is a request that exists, at a head the remote already holds, and both of
    those are things this run has only just made true.

    A dry run asks every question and answers none of them with a call that changes
    something. Reading the forge is not changing it, and reading is the only way to
    say which checks are required and whether the request is already armed - which
    is the half of the plan a captain would most want to look at before it happens.
    """
    if missing:
        # Only ever a dry run: both publication paths refuse a missing client before
        # they push. Reported and not refused, on the same terms the rest of a dry
        # run is - it stays readable on a machine that could not carry it out, and
        # says which half of the plan it could not read.
        print(f"automerge: {method} (the standing grant on this project)")
        print(f"  head    {accepted[:12]}, accepted by {qa_id}")
        print("  the client that would arrange it is not installed here, so nothing")
        print("  could be said about the request or the checks on it")
        return

    verdict, request, checks, refusal = automerge_state(
        forge, path, branch, target, method, accepted, pushed=not args.dry_run)

    if args.dry_run:
        print(f"automerge: {method} (the standing grant on this project)")
        print(f"  head    {accepted[:12]}, accepted by {qa_id}")
        print(f"  request {request['url'] if request else 'none open from ' + branch}")
        print(f"  checks  {show_checks(checks)}")
        if verdict == "merged":
            print("  it is merged already, so nothing would be armed")
        elif verdict == "armed":
            print(f"  it is armed already with {method}, so nothing would change")
        elif verdict == "arm":
            print(f"  would arm {method}, pinned to {accepted[:12]}, and the forge")
            print("  would merge it once every required check and review passes")
        else:
            print(f"  would not arm: {refusal[0]}")
            for hint in refusal[1:]:
                print(f"    {hint}")
        return

    if verdict == "refuse":
        # What the refusal is *not* about, said last because a nonzero exit here
        # would otherwise read as a publish that did not happen. Everything above
        # this line already landed: the branch is pushed and the request is carrying
        # the copy QA accepted. Only the arrangement is missing, and running this
        # again once the reason is gone is the whole of the recovery.
        landed = []
        if request is not None:
            landed = [f"the publish itself stands: {request['url']} is open and",
                      f"holds {accepted[:12]}; merging it is the captain's until",
                      "this is run again and arms it"]
        die(*refusal, *landed)
    if verdict == "merged":
        print(f"already merged: {request['url']}")
        print(f"  it holds {accepted[:12]}, which is the head {qa_id} accepted")
        return
    if verdict == "armed":
        print(f"already armed:  {request['url']}")
        print(f"  method  {method}, at {accepted[:12]}")
        print(f"  checks  {show_checks(checks)}")
        return

    arm(forge, path, branch, method, accepted)
    print(f"armed    {method} on {request['url']}")
    print(f"  head    {accepted[:12]}, accepted by {qa_id}")
    print(f"  checks  {show_checks(checks)}")
    print("  the forge merges it when every required check and review passes, and")
    print("  not before; cancel it with `siana-publish <qa-id> --cancel-automerge`")


def cancel_automerge(args, path: str, forge: str, branch: str, handle: str) -> int:
    """Disarm the request on this branch, and prove it is disarmed.

    Asks the registry nothing. Removing `automerge` stops the next publish from
    arming anything and cannot retract what is already armed at the forge, so the
    one sequence that revokes the grant safely is to cancel every armed request
    first, check that it took, and only then drop the field. A cancellation that
    needed the field still to be there would make that sequence impossible to
    carry out in the only order that is safe.

    Idempotent in both directions: no request, an open one that was never armed, and
    one this run has just disarmed all exit zero, because the end state is the same
    and a captain re-running this is asking about that state rather than about how
    it got there."""
    if forge not in AUTOMERGE:
        die(f"this fleet arranges no merges on {forge}, so there is none to cancel",
            f"nothing published from {handle} was ever armed here; if something is",
            "armed on that request, it was armed somewhere else and is cancelled",
            "there")

    found, problem = requests_for(forge, path, branch)
    if problem is not None:
        die(f"{forge} could not say what is open from {branch}", problem,
            "cancelling proves a request is no longer armed, and a forge that",
            "cannot be asked proves nothing")
    open_now = [r for r in found if r["state"].lower() in OPEN]
    if not open_now:
        print(f"nothing armed: no request is open from {branch}")
        return 0
    if len(open_now) > 1:
        die(f"{branch} is the source of more than one open request",
            *[f"  {r['url']}" for r in open_now],
            "which one to disarm is the captain's to say")
    request = open_now[0]

    if not request["armed"]:
        print(f"nothing armed: {request['url']}")
        return 0

    if args.dry_run:
        print(f"would cancel the armed merge of {request['url']}")
        print(f"  armed   {request['armed']}")
        print("  the request stays open, and merging it goes back to being the")
        print("  captain's, in person")
        return 0

    disarm(forge, path, branch)

    # Asked again, because a cancel that reported success and left the request armed
    # is the one outcome this command must never produce: the captain would then
    # remove the grant believing nothing can merge, and something still can.
    found, problem = requests_for(forge, path, branch)
    if problem is not None:
        die(f"{forge} would not say whether {branch} is still armed", problem,
            "the cancel was accepted, and nothing here can confirm it took; look",
            "at the request before removing the grant")
    still = [r for r in found
             if r["state"].lower() in OPEN and r["url"] == request["url"]]
    if still and still[0]["armed"]:
        die(f"{request['url']} is still armed with {still[0]['armed']}",
            "the cancel was accepted and did not take, so this request may still",
            "merge; cancel it in the forge's own interface before removing the",
            "grant")

    print(f"cancelled {request['url']}")
    print(f"  it was armed {request['armed']}, and is not armed now")
    print("  merging it is the captain's again, in person")
    return 0


def repair_publish(args, home: str, tasks_file: str, handle: str, path: str,
                   forge: str, branch: str, repair, qa_id: str, accepted: str,
                   title: str, body: str, method) -> None:
    """Advance the request this repair belongs on, and open nothing.

    A repair is cut from the branch a rejected or failing request was published from,
    and validated and judged on a branch of its own, because a minion may not work in
    a tree a live run or a live reviewer is reading. That isolation ends here: the
    accepted head is pushed to the request's own source branch, so the repair arrives
    on the thread the captain is already reading rather than as a second request
    holding the same commits.

    The request's copy is rewritten from the repair's own handoff afterwards, which
    the caller has already validated against `accepted`. What a reviewer reads has to
    describe the commits under it, and after this push those are the repair's; leaving
    the old copy standing would make the one run that changed the work the one run
    whose description never arrived.

    Everything below refuses before the push, which is the first thing here that
    leaves the machine. The push itself is an ordinary fast-forward: no force, no
    lease, no second request, and nothing about the request's number or target moves.

    Nothing here asks the advisory gate. `main` asks it above every check about this
    machine, so a gated run never reaches either publication path and this one has no
    second copy of that decision to keep right.
    """
    target_task, publication = repair
    cli = "glab" if forge == "gitlab" else "gh"
    missing = shutil.which(cli) is None

    # The head QA judged, and not merely the branch it was told to judge. A QA
    # worktree is cut from the repair branch when it is dispatched, so `siana/<qa-id>`
    # stands where that branch was when a second minion started reading it, and QA
    # lands nothing, so it is still there afterwards. Without this, a repair branch
    # that moved after the verdict would reach the captain's own request wearing a
    # green nobody gave that head.
    judged_at = ship_branch(home, qa_id)
    if git(path, "rev-parse", "--verify", judged_at, check=False).returncode != 0:
        die(f"{path} has no branch {judged_at}",
            f"it is where {qa_id} was cut from {branch}, and the only record of",
            "which head that verdict is about",
            "without it nothing here can say the branch has not moved since, and",
            "this pushes into a request the captain is already reading")
    judged = git(path, "rev-parse", judged_at).stdout.strip()
    if judged != accepted:
        die(f"{branch} is at {accepted[:12]}, and {qa_id} judged {judged[:12]}",
            "one of them moved after the verdict, so publishing this would put a",
            "head no independent minion has read on an open request",
            "look at both branches, then queue the repair again over what is there")

    live = held(tasks_file, handle, publication)
    if live:
        die(f"{publication} is checked out by {live[0]}, which is still running",
            f"held by {live[1].get('owner') or 'a minion'} at {live[1].get('cwd')}",
            "a push at a branch somebody is committing to leaves that minion",
            "diverged from the request it is working on, and it finds out at its",
            "own publish; wait for it, or reclaim the task first")

    plan = [f"branch:  {branch}",
            f"repairs: {target_task}, published from {publication}",
            f"head:    {accepted[:12]}, accepted by {qa_id}",
            f"forge:   {forge}"]

    def preview(*context):
        """What a dry run shows: the plan, then the exact copy that would go on.

        The body and not the title alone, the same as the ordinary path prints it.
        It matters more here: this rewrites the description of a request somebody is
        already reading, so a dry run showing only the title would preview the push
        and hide the overwrite - which is the half of a repair a human would want to
        look at before it happens."""
        for line in (*plan, *context):
            print(line)
        print(f"title:   {title}")
        print("body:")
        for line in body.splitlines():
            print(f"  {line}")

    if args.dry_run and missing:
        preview()
        print(f"would fast-forward {publication} to that head, on the request "
              f"already open from it")
        print(f"warning: {cli} is not installed here, so a real run would refuse",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return

    if missing:
        die(f"{cli} is not installed, and this needs it",
            "it is what says whether the request on that branch is still open, and",
            "advancing a branch whose request was closed or merged pushes work at",
            f"something nobody will read again; install {cli}")

    found, problem = requests_for(forge, path, publication)
    if problem is not None:
        die(f"{forge} could not say what is open from {publication}",
            problem,
            "this fast-forwards a request that exists, so a forge that cannot be",
            "asked is a refusal and never an assumption that there is none")
    open_now = [r for r in found if r["state"].lower() in OPEN]
    if not found:
        die(f"no merge request has ever been opened from {publication}",
            f"a repair of {target_task} lands on the request that work was",
            "published on, and there is none to land on",
            "publish the work it repairs first, or brief this as ordinary ship work")
    if not open_now:
        die(f"the request from {publication} is not open",
            *[f"  {r['state']}: {r['url']}" for r in found],
            "a closed or merged request is not a review thread any more, and its",
            "branch is not somewhere to push work nobody will read")
    if len(open_now) > 1:
        die(f"{publication} is the source of more than one open request",
            *[f"  {r['url']}" for r in open_now],
            "which one an accepted repair belongs on is the captain's to say, and",
            "not something a script may choose between")
    request = open_now[0]

    ls = git(path, "ls-remote", "origin", f"refs/heads/{publication}", check=False)
    if ls.returncode != 0:
        die(f"origin could not be read for {publication}",
            (ls.stderr or ls.stdout).strip(),
            "the remote head is what this proves a fast-forward against, so nothing",
            "is pushed until it can be read")
    if not ls.stdout.strip():
        die(f"origin has no {publication}",
            f"{request['url']} is open from it, so the branch it reads was deleted",
            "or renamed; there is nothing here to fast-forward")
    remote_head = ls.stdout.split()[0]

    # Asked before anything else about that head, because it is the answer that makes
    # re-running safe. Publishing happens outside the store, so a SIANA that restarts
    # between a verdict and this call cannot know whether it already ran; a branch
    # that is already at the accepted head is the end state either way, whatever the
    # forge's own record of it has caught up to yet.
    #
    # The copy is still put on, because the push and the update are two calls and a
    # run can end between them. Rewriting a description that already says this is
    # what it says again, so the recovery converges instead of needing to know how
    # far the interrupted run got.
    if remote_head == accepted:
        if args.dry_run:
            preview(f"request: {request['url']}",
                    f"remote:  {publication} is already at {accepted[:12]}")
            print("nothing would be pushed, and this copy would be put on again")
            return
        update(forge, path, publication, title, body)
        print(f"already advanced: {request['url']}")
        print(f"  {publication} is at {accepted[:12]}, which is the accepted head")
        print(f"  copy   updated to what {qa_id} accepted")
        return

    if request["head"] and request["head"] != remote_head:
        die(f"{publication} has moved since {request['url']} was read",
            f"the request is at {request['head'][:12]} and origin is at "
            f"{remote_head[:12]}",
            "somebody pushed to it, so what QA accepted was judged against a branch",
            "that is not the one this would advance; look before publishing again")

    # Fetched only when the commit is not here. It usually is: the repair was cut
    # from this branch, so the head it is being fast-forwarded from is an ancestor
    # of the work. A fetch is a read either way, and reading is what this is allowed
    # to do before the one push.
    if git(path, "cat-file", "-e", f"{remote_head}^{{commit}}",
           check=False).returncode != 0:
        git(path, "fetch", "--quiet", "origin", f"refs/heads/{publication}",
            check=False)
    if git(path, "cat-file", "-e", f"{remote_head}^{{commit}}",
           check=False).returncode != 0:
        die(f"{remote_head[:12]} is not here, and origin would not send it",
            f"it is what {publication} is at, and a fast-forward cannot be proved",
            "against a commit this repository does not have")

    ancestry = git(path, "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", remote_head, accepted,
                   check=False)
    if ancestry.returncode == 1:
        die(f"{accepted[:12]} is not a fast-forward of {publication}",
            f"origin is at {remote_head[:12]}, which the accepted head does not",
            "contain, so this would drop commits that are on the request already",
            "the repair has to be rebuilt over what the request holds now")
    if ancestry.returncode != 0:
        die(f"git could not compare {remote_head[:12]} and {accepted[:12]}",
            (ancestry.stderr or ancestry.stdout).strip())

    if args.dry_run:
        preview(f"request: {request['url']}",
                f"remote:  {publication} at {remote_head[:12]}")
        print(f"would push {accepted[:12]} to refs/heads/{publication} "
              f"(fast-forward, no force)")
        print("  no second request is opened, and its number and target do not move")
        return

    # The accepted commit by name, at the request's own source ref. The local
    # `publication` branch is deliberately not moved and not read: it is where the
    # work it repairs was left, and this is about what the request holds.
    push = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "-C", path, "push", "origin", f"{accepted}:refs/heads/{publication}"],
        capture_output=True, text=True)
    if push.returncode != 0:
        die(f"pushing {accepted[:12]} to {publication} failed",
            (push.stderr or push.stdout).strip(),
            "nothing else was done, so fixing this and running again is safe")

    # After the push and not before it. The two are separate calls either way, so one
    # of the orders leaves a description of commits that are not there yet and the
    # other leaves commits under a description that has not caught up. The second is
    # the safer half-done state, and it is the one a re-run repairs above.
    update(forge, path, publication, title, body)

    print(f"advanced {publication} to {accepted[:12]}")
    print(f"  on     {request['url']}")
    print(f"  from   {branch}, accepted by {qa_id}")
    print(f"  copy   updated to what {qa_id} accepted")
    # Under a grant the second half of this would be false, and the arming step the
    # caller runs next says what is true instead. The first half holds either way,
    # and is the guarantee a repair exists to make.
    if method:
        print("  no second merge request was opened")
    else:
        print("  no second merge request was opened, and merging is still the "
              "captain's")


def propose(task_id: str, record: str) -> int:
    """Ask `siana-gate` whether this may happen, and let it say so in its own words.

    Resolved beside this script rather than found on PATH: a `siana-gate` that is not
    there must be a refusal and never a publish that went ahead because a lookup came
    back empty. Its output is not captured, so the decision id and the reason reach
    whoever is reading, and its exit code is passed through, so a refusal and a fault
    stay distinguishable all the way up."""
    gate = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "siana-gate")
    if not os.path.isfile(gate):
        die(f"an advisory session is in force and there is no {gate}",
            "it is the only thing that answers whether this may happen, so without",
            "it nothing is known and nothing is published",
            "run `just init` in the distro")
    out = subprocess.run([gate, "publish", "--task", task_id, "--record", record])
    if out.returncode != 0:
        print("nothing was pushed and no merge request was opened", file=sys.stderr)
    return out.returncode


def main() -> None:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description="Publish the branch a QA task accepted: a merge request of its "
                    "own, or a fast-forward of the one it repairs.")
    ap.add_argument("task_id", help="the QA task whose verdict authorises this")
    ap.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true",
                    help="print what would be published, and change nothing")
    ap.add_argument("--cancel-automerge", action="store_true",
                    help="disarm the merge arranged for this work, and prove it is "
                         "disarmed; needs no `automerge` in the registry, because "
                         "cancelling before the field is removed is the only safe "
                         "order to revoke the grant in")
    ap.add_argument("--record", metavar="FILE",
                    help="the decision SIANA wrote, as JSON; required while an "
                         "advisory session is in force, and a request to be gated "
                         "whenever it is given")
    args = ap.parse_args()

    home = os.environ.get("SIANA_HOME", os.path.expanduser("~/.siana"))
    tasks_file = os.environ.get("SIANA_TASKS_FILE", os.path.join(home, "tasks.jsonl"))
    registry = os.path.join(home, "projects.jsonl")

    # Whether this publish is gated at all. Two things say so, and either is enough.
    #
    # A session record being there is the ordinary one. Only its presence is asked
    # here: whether the process behind it is alive, whether the deadline has passed,
    # and whether the principles still hash to what it recorded are `siana-gate`'s to
    # settle, and a second copy of that check here would be a second thing to keep
    # right. A record whose process is gone therefore reaches the gate and is refused
    # there, which is correct: it is a session the captain has to read and clear, and
    # until they do, nothing publishes on the strength of a file nobody has looked at.
    #
    # `--record` being given is the other, and it is not a redundant one. A caller
    # writes a decision record only because it believes it is under a session, and
    # the case where that belief is wrong is the case that matters: the deadline
    # passes at 06:00 and `siana-afk` releases the record, SIANA wakes at 06:01 with
    # a proposal already written, and a publish that ignored the flag would push the
    # branch and open the merge request with nothing in the ledger. Reading the flag
    # as the request it is costs nothing, because with no session the gate records
    # the proposal and refuses, which is exactly what the caller was asking for.
    gated = args.record is not None or os.path.exists(os.path.join(home, "afk"))
    # A dry run is exempt, because it changes nothing and records nothing either. A
    # proposal written for a command that was never going to run would put a decision
    # in front of the captain that SIANA did not make.
    if gated and not args.record and not args.dry_run:
        die("an advisory session is in force, so this publish needs --record",
            "the record is what SIANA read, what it rejected, and which principle it",
            "is citing, and it is what the captain reads in the morning instead of",
            "a merge request that appeared while they were asleep",
            "  siana-publish <qa-task-id> --record <file>",
            "stop the session with `siana-afk --stop` if you are here and want this",
            "published now")

    task = fold(tasks_file, "id").get(args.task_id)
    if task is None:
        die(f"no task {args.task_id} in {tasks_file}",
            "publishing is authorised by a verdict, so a task that is not in the",
            "queue authorises nothing")

    # Every refusal below is a way the branch could be published without a second
    # minion having accepted it, which is the one thing this script exists to make
    # impossible.
    if task.get("status") != "done":
        die(f"{args.task_id} is {task.get('status')}, not done",
            "a verdict that has not come back is not a pass, and `blocked` is a",
            "finding: queue the fix with `--base` on the branch it judged")

    branch = base = task.get("base")
    if not branch:
        die(f"{args.task_id} names no base, so it judged no branch",
            "a QA task carries the branch it was cut from; a task without one is",
            "ship or scout work, and neither authorises a publish")

    handle = task.get("project")
    if not handle:
        die(f"{args.task_id} names no project",
            "the project is what says where the repository is and what a merge",
            "request targets")

    if not os.path.isfile(os.path.join(home, "schema-projects.yaml")):
        die(f"no project registry at {registry}", "run `just init` in the distro")
    projects = fold(registry, "handle")
    if handle not in projects:
        known = ", ".join(sorted(projects)) or "(the registry is empty)"
        die(f"unknown project: {handle}", f"known handles: {known}")
    project = projects[handle]

    target = project.get("target")
    # Cancelling needs no target. It disarms a request that already exists, and the
    # captain revoking this grant may well have taken `target` off the record in the
    # same sitting - which must not be the thing that leaves an armed merge standing.
    if not target and not args.cancel_automerge:
        die(f"publishing is off for {handle}",
            "`target` is the branch a merge request targets, and setting it is what",
            "turns publishing on:",
            f"  datafile -f {registry} get {handle}   # then put it back",
            "  ... --set target=<branch>")

    # The ship task is what the merge request is about. A QA task depends on exactly
    # the task it judges, so the dependency is the pointer and nothing has to be
    # threaded onto the record.
    deps = task.get("deps") or []
    if not deps:
        die(f"{args.task_id} depends on nothing, so it names no ship task",
            "a QA task is queued with `--dep <ship-id>`; without it there is no",
            "brief to describe the work and no title to give the merge request")
    ship_id = deps[0]

    # `base` means two opposite things depending on what the task is. On a QA task it
    # is the branch being judged; on a ship task cut from existing work it is the
    # branch the minion started from and then committed past. Publishing the second
    # one ships the state before the work rather than after it, and the record alone
    # cannot tell them apart - the live queue holds `done` ship tasks carrying both a
    # base and a dep, in exactly the shape a QA task has.
    #
    # What separates them is that a QA task is cut from the branch of the very task
    # it waits on. A fix task waits on the QA verdict that found the defect while
    # being cut from the ship branch, so the two never line up.
    #
    # The branch of the task it waits on is read off that task's brief and never
    # rebuilt from its id: since ship branches carry the commit type SIANA stated,
    # `siana/<ship-id>` is a name only legacy work has, and comparing against it
    # would refuse every typed QA verdict as if it were a ship task.
    judged = ship_branch(home, ship_id)
    brief_path = os.path.join(home, "briefs", f"{ship_id}.md")
    # A base under `siana/` ending in this ship task's own id is a branch cut for
    # that task whatever type it carries. So when one of those does not match, and
    # the brief the type would have been read from is not there, what is missing is
    # the brief and not the task: "not a QA task" would put a false statement about
    # the queue in place of a true one about a file, and the two are fixed
    # differently. A base naming some other task stays the wrong-task refusal, which
    # is what it is.
    for_this_task = base.startswith("siana/") and base.rsplit("/", 1)[-1] == ship_id
    if base != judged and for_this_task and not os.path.isfile(brief_path):
        die(f"no brief at {brief_path}",
            f"{args.task_id} was cut from {base}, and without that brief nothing",
            f"here can say which branch {ship_id} built: the fallback is {judged},",
            "which is the name only work briefed before commit types carries",
            "a verdict about a branch this cannot identify authorises nothing")
    if base != judged:
        die(f"{args.task_id} is not a QA task",
            f"it waits on {ship_id} but was cut from {base}, and a QA task is cut",
            f"from the branch of the task it judges ({judged})",
            "on a ship task `base` is where the minion started, not what it built,",
            "so publishing it would ship the state before the work",
            "pass the QA task whose verdict authorises this instead")

    if fold(tasks_file, "id").get(ship_id) is None:
        die(f"{args.task_id} depends on {ship_id}, which is not in the queue")

    # Whether this is a repair of published work, stated by SIANA when it briefed the
    # fix and read here off that brief's own record. Nothing infers it: a branch cut
    # from another one is the ordinary shape of every fix, so ancestry, a title, a
    # commit message or a request that happens to be open over there are all things
    # that would make ordinary work land on somebody else's review thread.
    #
    # Read with the rest of the queue's refusals, before anything about this machine
    # is asked, because a record nothing can read one repair out of is a brief to fix
    # rather than a publish to attempt.
    repair = repair_record(home, ship_id)

    # The queue's refusals are all above: they say this verdict does not authorise a
    # publish, and that is true whoever is asking. This asks who is asking, and it
    # comes before everything below because all of that is about whether this machine
    # could carry the publish out - a different question, and not one an advisory
    # night needs answered. A proposal has to be recorded on a machine whose registry
    # path has been moved, whose repository has no `origin`, whose remote is on a
    # forge nobody here recognises, or that has no forge CLI installed at all. Each
    # of those exited before this was asked, and a decision SIANA reached was
    # discarded with nothing in the ledger - which is the one failure that reads in
    # the morning as a quiet night.
    #
    # A dry run is exempt, and exempt here rather than by falling through the block
    # below, for the same reason it needs no record: it changes nothing, and a
    # proposal written for a command that was never going to run would put a decision
    # in front of the captain that SIANA did not make.
    if gated and not args.dry_run:
        raise SystemExit(propose(args.task_id, args.record))

    # What follows asks whether the world can carry the publish out, which is a
    # different failure from any above and worth reaching in a different order.
    path = os.path.abspath(os.path.expanduser(str(project["path"])))
    if not os.path.isdir(path):
        die(f"{handle} points at {path}, which is not a directory",
            f"fix the `path` for {handle} in {registry}")

    if git(path, "rev-parse", "--git-dir", check=False).returncode != 0:
        die(f"{handle} is at {path}, which is not a git repository",
            "there is no branch to push and nothing to open a merge request from")

    at = git(path, "rev-parse", "--verify", f"{branch}^{{commit}}", check=False)
    if at.returncode != 0:
        die(f"{path} has no branch {branch}",
            "the work this verdict accepted is not there under that name; it may",
            "have been landed and deleted, or the worktree removed with it",
            "look before deciding: a verdict about a branch nobody can find is",
            "not evidence about anything that exists now")
    # The head QA accepted, and the whole of what binds the copy below to this work:
    # the handoff records the commit it describes, so a document written before a
    # later commit refuses here instead of describing something that has moved.
    head = at.stdout.strip()

    remote = git(path, "remote", "get-url", "origin", check=False)
    if remote.returncode != 0:
        die(f"{handle} has no `origin` remote at {path}",
            "there is nowhere to push, so there is no merge request to open")
    remote_url = remote.stdout.strip()
    forge = forge_of(remote_url)
    if forge is None:
        die(f"cannot tell which forge {remote_url} is",
            "only gitlab and github are recognised, and the wrong guess publishes",
            "somewhere nobody asked for; open this one by hand")

    cli = "glab" if forge == "gitlab" else "gh"
    missing = shutil.which(cli) is None

    # The branch whose request carries this work: its own, or the one it repairs.
    # Both publication paths end on it, and it is the only branch anything is armed
    # or disarmed on.
    publication = publication_branch(home, ship_id)

    # Cancelling stops here. It asks for no target, no handoff, no pipeline record
    # and no grant, because every one of those is a condition for arming and none of
    # them is a condition for taking an arming back. It reaches this line rather than
    # an earlier one so that the queue's refusals and the advisory gate above have
    # already had their say: a verdict that authorises no publish authorises no
    # cancellation either, and a session that forbids arming forbids disarming.
    if args.cancel_automerge:
        if missing:
            die(f"{cli} is not installed, and cancelling needs it",
                "this proves the request is no longer armed rather than assuming",
                f"it, and there is nothing here to ask; install {cli}")
        raise SystemExit(cancel_automerge(args, path, forge, publication, handle))

    # Whether the captain has granted this project a merge, and by which method. Read
    # before the copy is assembled, so a grant this machine cannot honour refuses on
    # what is wrong with the registry rather than on the first handoff rule the run
    # happens to reach afterwards.
    method = project.get("automerge")
    if method:
        automerge_grant(home, registry, handle, project, forge, ship_id, branch,
                        head)

    # Before the dry run, because a dry run whose job is to show the exact title and
    # body cannot show them without this. Before the gate, because a proposal is a
    # record of a publish SIANA was ready to carry out, and one whose copy does not
    # assemble was never that.
    title, body = copy(ship_id, head, args.task_id)

    # Two publications, and the record on the brief is what says which. Ordinary ship
    # work pushes its own branch and opens a request from it; a repair advances the
    # request the work it repairs is already being reviewed on, and opens nothing.
    #
    # Read here rather than earlier so that everything above - the verdict, the
    # branch, the project, the forge, the copy - has already been checked the same
    # way for both. A repair is not a different authorisation, only a different
    # destination.
    if repair is not None:
        repair_publish(args, home, tasks_file, handle, path, forge, branch, repair,
                       args.task_id, head, title, body, method)
        # On the branch the request is open from, and not on the repair's own. A
        # repair re-establishes every condition over there from scratch - the request
        # is at a head it reached one call ago, and the checks on it are the checks
        # of that head - so the round that fixed a red request arms the same way the
        # round that opened it did.
        if method:
            automerge_step(args, path, forge, repair[1], target, method, head,
                           args.task_id, missing)
        return

    if args.dry_run:
        print(f"branch:  {branch}")
        print(f"target:  {target}")
        print(f"forge:   {forge} ({remote_url})")
        print(f"title:   {title}")
        print("body:")
        for line in body.splitlines():
            print(f"  {line}")
        # Reported rather than refused. A dry run changes nothing, so it stays
        # readable on any machine; it just says what a real one would hit.
        if missing:
            print(f"warning: {cli} is not installed here, so a real run would refuse",
                  file=sys.stderr)
        if method:
            automerge_step(args, path, forge, branch, target, method, head,
                           args.task_id, missing)
        return

    # Checked before the push, not after it. A missing CLI discovered later leaves
    # the branch published with no merge request and nothing saying why.
    if missing:
        die(f"{cli} is not installed, and {remote_url} needs it",
            "the branch would push and then have nowhere to open a merge request,",
            f"which is the half-done state this refuses to create; install {cli}")

    # Asked before the push, and only where a merge was granted. Under the grant the
    # request this publish opened can have merged since, which every step below reads
    # as a branch with no open request and would answer by opening a second one for
    # commits that have already landed. Nowhere else has this problem: without the
    # grant nothing merges between two runs unless the captain merged it, and then
    # they are not re-running this.
    if method:
        kind, request, _ = one_request(forge, path, branch, head)
        if kind == "merged":
            print(f"already merged: {request['url']}")
            print(f"  it holds {head[:12]}, which is the head {args.task_id} "
                  f"accepted")
            return

    push = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", path, "push", "--set-upstream", "origin", branch],
                          capture_output=True, text=True)
    if push.returncode != 0:
        die(f"pushing {branch} to origin failed",
            (push.stderr or push.stdout).strip(),
            "nothing was opened, so fixing this and running again is safe")

    open_mr = existing(forge, path, branch)
    if open_mr:
        update(forge, path, branch, title, body)
        print(f"already open: {open_mr}")
        print(f"  copy   updated to what {args.task_id} accepted")
        print(f"  branch {branch} pushed (no second merge request opened)")
    else:
        url = create(forge, path, branch, target, title, body)
        print(f"opened   {url}")
        print(f"  branch {branch} -> {target}")
        # Without the grant this is the whole of what this command has ever said
        # about merging, and under one it would be false, so the arming step below
        # says what is true instead.
        if not method:
            print("  merging is the captain's, and still done by hand")

    # Last, because everything it proves is about a request that exists at a head the
    # remote already holds, and both of those are what the lines above have only just
    # made true.
    if method:
        automerge_step(args, path, forge, branch, target, method, head,
                       args.task_id, missing)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
